\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Observables on a finite configuration set}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Let #{V} be a finite vertex set and #{S} a finite set of possible
values. A configuration is a function #{\sigma:V\to S}; the configuration
set is #{\Omega=S^V}. The set #{\Omega} is distinct from the vertex set
#{V}: an observable takes a whole configuration as its input. For a
commutative unital ring #{R}, set
##{A_\Omega=\operatorname{Map}(\Omega,R).}
This is the \vocabk{pointwise function algebra}{fdip-0010}:
#{(f+g)(\sigma)=f(\sigma)+g(\sigma)},
#{(fg)(\sigma)=f(\sigma)g(\sigma)} and
#{(rf)(\sigma)=rf(\sigma)}, with unit the constant function #{1_R}.}

\p{For each #{\sigma\in\Omega}, evaluation
#{\operatorname{ev}_\sigma(f)=f(\sigma)} is a unital #{R}-algebra map
and a [normalized linear functional](fdip-0021).
The scalar map sends #{r} to the constant function #{r}.
The indicator #{\delta_\sigma} is #{1_R} at #{\sigma} and #{0_R}
elsewhere. These are the same indicators used in
[counting duality for finite functions](fdip-001F), now indexed by configurations.}
\proof{
\p{Each algebra law follows by evaluating at every configuration and
using the corresponding law in #{R}. Evaluation preserves addition,
scalar multiplication, products and the unit by the displayed definitions.
The indicators satisfy
##{\delta_\sigma^2=\delta_\sigma,\qquad
\delta_\sigma\delta_\tau=0\ (\sigma\ne\tau),\qquad
\sum_{\sigma\in\Omega}\delta_\sigma=1.}
These equalities hold pointwise, including when #{R} is the zero ring.}
}
\p{If #{V=\varnothing}, then #{S^V} contains the unique empty function,
even when #{S=\varnothing}. If #{S=\varnothing} and #{V\ne\varnothing},
then #{\Omega=\varnothing}; its function algebra is the zero algebra.
There is then no evaluation at a configuration.}

\p{The underlying unital algebra convention is
\citet{Section 22.2, Tag 09JF}{stacks2017stacks}.
Mathlib's \leanref{Pi.algebra}, \leanref{Pi.evalAlgHom} and
\leanref{Pi.constAlgHom} provide the pointwise algebra, evaluation and
constant algebra maps. The configuration interpretation and the empty-set
cases above are direct consequences of these definitions.}
